 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "Single case report of a 6-year-old boy with a GFAP variant: \"revealed a novel pathogenic GFAP gene mutation\".",
 "method_note": "Read in full by hand; output assembled with a short Python script from manually chosen values (no table transcription; the paper has no tables). The variant is printed only as prose ('C>T nucleotide substitution in exon 4 at amino acid position 258'); no c./p. notation or transcript is printed, so the HGVS fields are null and variant type is 'not reported' (missense is not stated). Inheritance/parental testing not stated. Onset is taken as the first symptom (intermittent vomiting at 3 years); the paper does not explicitly call it disease onset. Age at diagnosis not stated as such (the 6-year age is recorded as age at last evaluation). Normal findings handled per D-27: normal head circumference -> macrocephaly and microcephaly excluded; normal celiac testing -> positive celiac testing excluded; normal fourth ventricle -> abnormal fourth ventricle excluded. 'Laboratory evaluation was normal' and 'neurologic exam was normal except...' are whole-examination statements and exclude nothing more specific (no checklist items excluded from them). Checklist 'macrocephaly or megalencephaly' left not reported (normal head circumference addresses only the narrower macrocephaly). 'No other lesions were seen within the brain parenchyma' used to exclude the cerebral white matter, periventricular rim and basal ganglia/thalamic checklist items. Mild hyperreflexia counted as a pyramidal sign (observed). One previously published person from the Discussion is included under the v4 rule ('ref 11 patient', a child with a tumor-like optic chiasm lesion, ref [11] Mignot 2009); other cited cases (refs 7-10) are mentioned only as groups and get no rows. No contradictions found.",
